WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.109
Should we be worried about the singularity?

00:00:08.109 --> 00:00:11.786
Oh boy, this gets me into all kinds of interesting

00:00:11.786 --> 00:00:14.669
So First question is,

00:00:14.669 --> 00:00:20.485
we have to look at the likely future of artificial intelligence and all these kinds of things is.

00:00:20.485 --> 00:00:25.529
. I think we can see lots of trends right now, we can ask questions.

00:00:25.529 --> 00:00:29.999
Are we all going to be eaten by AI’s, these kinds of things?

00:00:29.999 --> 00:00:33.990
I think the story of technology

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:38.831
has been a sort of a story of how do we automate things that humans do.

00:00:38.831 --> 00:00:42.575
How do we take &amp; ultimately, what does technology do?

00:00:42.575 --> 00:00:48.957
What does technology should do is take what humans want to do &amp; have it happen automatically.

00:00:48.957 --> 00:00:53.204
That’s what we try to do with Wolfram language &amp; with our technology stack

00:00:53.204 --> 00:00:57.373
is have the humans tell us what they want to do. That’s what Wolfram Language is about.

00:00:57.373 --> 00:01:00.735
Then we’ll go figure out how best to do that.

00:01:00.735 --> 00:01:07.655
So in a sense as AI gets better, as our capabilities in these directions get better,

00:01:07.655 --> 00:01:11.625
we will get better at the automating the execution of human goals.

00:01:11.625 --> 00:01:14.915
One of the issues that’s sort of interesting is how do you describe your goals.

00:01:14.915 --> 00:01:17.004
How do you describe what you want to do to a system?

00:01:17.004 --> 00:01:20.684
We’ve got kind of 2 answers to that, one is in Wolfram Alpha

00:01:20.684 --> 00:01:22.405
you use pure human natural language.

00:01:22.405 --> 00:01:27.247
The other is in Wolfram Language where you’re using a precise specification language.

00:01:27.247 --> 00:01:33.645
It’s sort of interesting to me to think about what kinds of things can you just describe in pure natural language?

00:01:33.645 --> 00:01:36.594
What kinds of things do you need a more structured language to describe?

00:01:36.594 --> 00:01:40.623
It’s kind of interesting in writing this little book about Wolfram Language I’ve been doing,

00:01:40.623 --> 00:01:43.571
and I’ve been trying to write exercises and things for this book.

00:01:43.571 --> 00:01:48.586
As I get later &amp; later in the book so people sort of presumably know more and more about Wolfram Language.

00:01:48.586 --> 00:01:52.789
I’m writing these exercises, I’m realizing there’s something incredibly goofy about what I’m doing.

00:01:52.789 --> 00:01:56.871
I’m trying to describe in English something which is actually a fairly short program.

00:01:56.871 --> 00:01:59.946
I’m writing these several sentence English things.

00:01:59.946 --> 00:02:04.887
Then I see program this corresponds to is this really short thing that’s pretty easy to understand.

00:02:04.887 --> 00:02:08.432
So there’s a case where natural language is not the most efficient way to interact.

00:02:08.432 --> 00:02:13.738
OK so with respect to kind of AI’s I tend to think

00:02:13.738 --> 00:02:19.171
So the first thing to understand is you know there’s, well

00:02:19.171 --> 00:02:21.922
Let’s talk about what technology can do.

00:02:21.922 --> 00:02:24.176
I mean, so technology

00:02:24.176 --> 00:02:31.171
so I believe there is a certain scenario in which the machines in a sense take over, but in a rather strange way.

00:02:31.171 --> 00:02:35.074
You know when we drive around with cars with GPS’s and so on

00:02:35.074 --> 00:02:37.662
most of us like me for example, they just plug in an address

00:02:37.662 --> 00:02:41.391
and GPS tells them where to go and then just drive wherever the GPS tells us to go.

00:02:41.391 --> 00:02:45.478
Increasingly, systems will be able to predict what we should do

00:02:45.478 --> 00:02:48.470
based on our sort of stated goal or whatever

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:50.700
systems will predict what we should do this, we should do that.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:55.235
You’re talking to this person, you know this person has these interests which overlap with yours.

00:02:55.235 --> 00:02:58.149
You should raise this point in the conversation etc., etc., etc.

00:02:58.149 --> 00:03:03.631
All these kinds of things, and I think that the much likely scenario in the fairly near term,

00:03:03.631 --> 00:03:08.191
is you know there’s enough auto suggestion from the machines that most humans,

00:03:08.191 --> 00:03:11.723
most of the time are just doing what their machines are suggesting to them to do.

00:03:11.723 --> 00:03:15.122
So that’s in some sense, that’s the kind of you know,

00:03:15.122 --> 00:03:16.886
then you know the machines are in charge in some sense

00:03:16.886 --> 00:03:20.578
although only by the choice of the people that they’re doing that.

00:03:20.578 --> 00:03:24.258
But I think more generally, you know one of the questions is

00:03:24.258 --> 00:03:29.851
imagine that you have sort of the infinitely smart machine and it’s sitting in a box on your desk.

00:03:29.851 --> 00:03:31.627
Well I should explain another point, the

00:03:31.627 --> 00:03:39.327
ok so there’s this question about what you know sort of scales of computation and scales of intelligence and so on.

00:03:39.327 --> 00:03:42.263
Well I used to believe that there was sort of a,

00:03:42.263 --> 00:03:47.115
that there were things that were just computation and then there were things that were intelligence.

00:03:47.115 --> 00:03:50.250
I used to assume that there was kind of a bright line distinction

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:54.268
between the merely computational and the actually intelligent so to speak

00:03:54.268 --> 00:03:58.713
Then I spent a bunch of years working on my big New Kind of Science effort

00:03:58.713 --> 00:04:00.721
and I realized that was just not true.

00:04:00.721 --> 00:04:05.792
That in fact there is, when we look at different computational systems

00:04:05.792 --> 00:04:09.658
there’s a certain threshold that’s reached that we can start seeing

00:04:09.658 --> 00:04:12.350
in things like phenomenon of universal computation and so on.

00:04:12.350 --> 00:04:17.945
Above that threshold basically all systems are equivalent in the computations they can do.

00:04:17.945 --> 00:04:20.850
So kind of the funny version of that is things like you know,

00:04:20.850 --> 00:04:23.543
the weather has a mind of its own. What does that mean?

00:04:23.543 --> 00:04:29.325
Well, it kind of, that actually has some sort of scientific meaning

00:04:29.325 --> 00:04:32.434
because you know these fluid dynamics in the atmosphere and so on

00:04:32.434 --> 00:04:35.707
is doing a computation just like things in our brains are doing a computation.

00:04:35.707 --> 00:04:39.117
Question is can we say our brains are just dramatically much better

00:04:39.117 --> 00:04:41.034
than the fluid in the atmosphere and the answer is no.

00:04:41.034 --> 00:04:44.322
They’re both doing sort of an irreducible amount of computation.

00:04:44.322 --> 00:04:51.171
Throughout, in lots of different places in nature and elsewhere

00:04:51.171 --> 00:04:52.901
and in little programs that we can construct

00:04:52.901 --> 00:04:57.801
we’re able to make things which sort of have as sophisticated computation as any other thing.

00:04:57.801 --> 00:05:03.091
That has all kinds of consequences about making models and science and all sorts of other things.

00:05:03.091 --> 00:05:06.376
But one consequence it has is that one no longer feels,

00:05:06.376 --> 00:05:10.449
I don’t think one can feel sort of that the human intelligence thing is special

00:05:10.449 --> 00:05:12.691
relative to what’s possible in computation elsewhere.

00:05:12.691 --> 00:05:17.617
So this kind of philosophical realization was actually the thing

00:05:17.617 --> 00:05:21.157
that caused me to actually launch into trying to do the Wolfram Alpha project,

00:05:21.157 --> 00:05:24.454
about whatever it was, 12 years ago or something now.

00:05:24.454 --> 00:05:29.237
Cause I’d always assumed I’d kind of thought about doing things like Wolfram Alpha for twenty years before that.

00:05:29.237 --> 00:05:34.051
But I’d always assumed that to build Wolfram Alpha, to build a knowledge engine

00:05:34.051 --> 00:05:42.344
that could deal with lots of computable knowledge in the world that one would have to have a human brain like thing.

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:52.306
One would have to do something that emulated a brain. I realized from working on ks

00:05:52.306 --> 00:05:55.732
that basically it’s really, it’s all just computation.

00:05:55.732 --> 00:05:59.419
There’s no sharp distinction between the intelligent and the merely computational.

00:05:59.419 --> 00:06:07.407
So anyways, then the question is ok so you end up with the AI box on your desk

00:06:07.407 --> 00:06:10.436
and it’s doing all the sophisticated computation inside.

00:06:10.436 --> 00:06:16.995
The big problem for an AI box is well, you know it can do the computation.

00:06:16.995 --> 00:06:20.588
The computation it does is equivalent to a brain, its equivalent to lots of systems in nature.

00:06:20.588 --> 00:06:23.152
The question is, what does it choose to do?

00:06:23.152 --> 00:06:28.562
The thing is that while, you know the one feature of what we end up doing that doesn’t

00:06:28.562 --> 00:06:30.918
there’s no sort of theoretical sense of the,

00:06:30.918 --> 00:06:32.679
the box must have these goals.

00:06:32.679 --> 00:06:37.987
The box, there isn’t a, there’s no abstract way of defining goals for things.

00:06:37.987 --> 00:06:41.841
Goals are things that arise for humans from sort of a long history,

00:06:41.841 --> 00:06:46.497
of you know cultural history, individual history, and biology etc.etc.etc.

00:06:46.497 --> 00:06:51.548
So I think that, it’ sort of nonsense to imagine that there’s this AI,

00:06:51.548 --> 00:06:53.355
that’s it’s just doing its own thing.

00:06:53.355 --> 00:07:01.322
It’s something where ultimately, lots of things do computation but for the thing to have goals,

00:07:01.322 --> 00:07:03.795
goals are a very human kind of thing.

00:07:03.795 --> 00:07:06.161
So anyway, there’s that sort of thing.

00:07:06.161 --> 00:07:09.226
I think that the, oh gosh there’s so much to say about this.

00:07:09.226 --> 00:07:10.813
I’ve thought a whole bunch about this.

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:17.361
Another thing that’s sort of interesting,

00:07:17.361 --> 00:07:24.770
as we look at kind of the emergence of things that used to be pure human ability.

00:07:24.770 --> 00:07:27.824
Like for example, things like image recognition.

00:07:27.824 --> 00:07:31.318
That used to be something humans found easy, but oh computers find it really hard.

00:07:31.318 --> 00:07:35.994
Well, now it turns out we’ve managed to get to the point that computers find it pretty easy as well.

00:07:35.994 --> 00:07:41.009
One thing that’s interesting, is the reason computers find it easy now

00:07:41.009 --> 00:07:45.328
is because with our CPU’s and GPU’s and so on we’ve reached a scale

00:07:45.328 --> 00:07:48.393
where it’s somewhat comparable to scale of human visual processing,

00:07:48.393 --> 00:07:51.666
to the scale of what we do in our own brains for visual processing.

00:07:54.565 --> 00:07:59.200
So that’s kind of why we’re impressed now because our machines can identify ten thousand kinds of objects,

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:02.141
which is about the same as what we can identify.

00:08:02.141 --> 00:08:05.760
So then the question comes when what happens when the machines get bigger?

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:09.461
Which they inevitably will, and they can identify a million kinds of objects.

00:08:09.461 --> 00:08:11.086
Far beyond what we can do

00:08:11.086 --> 00:08:13.143
How do we think about that?

00:08:13.143 --> 00:08:18.444
What's going on, and one of the things to realize is that the machine

00:08:18.444 --> 00:08:23.886
So the thing that we have to communicate,

00:08:23.886 --> 00:08:27.485
when we have thoughts the way that we get to communicate them

00:08:27.485 --> 00:08:29.171
is with natural language

00:08:29.171 --> 00:08:33.899
I should say that I sort of view there of being some kind of hierarchy and I don't fully understand this

00:08:33.899 --> 00:08:36.227
so I'm not going to be totally coherent on this

00:08:36.227 --> 00:08:43.692
but there is sort of a hierarchy of communication mechanisms that exists in world so to speak,

00:08:43.692 --> 00:08:47.532
so sort of the lowest level is you're an organism

00:08:47.532 --> 00:08:52.791
and you're going to have progeny and there's sort of communication from you to your progeny

00:08:52.791 --> 00:08:56.756
because you have shared genetics and certain information is communicated

00:08:56.756 --> 00:08:59.936
by the fact that, by genetics basically

00:08:59.936 --> 00:09:03.355
The next level of communication so to speak

00:09:03.355 --> 00:09:07.706
is the kind of thing that happens in physiological recognition,

00:09:07.706 --> 00:09:13.111
where in every generation there is a fresh brain and that brain is exposed to the world

00:09:13.111 --> 00:09:16.364
and that brain learns object recognition for example

00:09:16.364 --> 00:09:21.028
well then the next thing which was sort of the big innovation of a few hundred thousand years ago

00:09:21.028 --> 00:09:24.598
of our species was this idea of natural language

00:09:24.598 --> 00:09:31.415
the idea that you could go from one brain through natural language to communicate pure thoughts to another brain

00:09:31.415 --> 00:09:36.713
and that's kind of the big innovation that led to human civilization

00:09:36.713 --> 00:09:38.909
and all kinds of other good things

00:09:38.909 --> 00:09:42.124
so the thing that I'm wondering and I don't understand it yet

00:09:42.124 --> 00:09:45.541
is, so ok we have a different form of communication

00:09:45.541 --> 00:09:49.746
so one disadvantage of natural language is I tell you something

00:09:49.746 --> 00:09:53.356
and I've got a well formed thought in my brain,

00:09:53.356 --> 00:09:56.258
but to communicate it to you I have to go through natural language

00:09:56.258 --> 00:09:59.596
you have to absorb the thing., you have to turn it into a thought in your brain

00:09:59.596 --> 00:10:00.571
it's all kind of inefficient

00:10:00.571 --> 00:10:05.384
By the time we have a knowledge based language computer language

00:10:05.384 --> 00:10:09.446
that can describe the same kinds of things that natural language can describe

00:10:09.446 --> 00:10:11.440
that can talk about the real world and so on

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:15.407
that it doesn't have this feature, that it has to be reabsorbed by another brain

00:10:15.407 --> 00:10:19.892
it's something where the language itself precisely defines what to do

00:10:19.892 --> 00:10:23.925
you can just send it to any interpreter of the language and it can immediately execute it

00:10:23.925 --> 00:10:27.105
so  you have a kind of closer form of communication

00:10:27.105 --> 00:10:31.830
than is possible by any of these other levels in this sort of hierarchy

00:10:31.830 --> 00:10:35.381
I don't understand what the consequences of these are but I think they may be rather significant

00:10:35.381 --> 00:10:43.131
and this raises questions like, when a large fraction of people in the world know how to program,

00:10:43.131 --> 00:10:47.670
what consequences does that have, what kinds of new forms of organization become possible

00:10:47.670 --> 00:10:49.406
so things like that

00:10:49.406 --> 00:10:55.140
But another thing that I realized is that we talk about human natural language

00:10:55.140 --> 00:11:00.237
as a way that we communicate and we reason about the world and so on

00:11:00.237 --> 00:11:02.459
One of the things that is a little troubling to me is

00:11:02.459 --> 00:11:06.785
when we talk about sort of AI systems that recognize a million kinds of objects and so on

00:11:06.785 --> 00:11:11.239
inside that system, it is making distinctions, it's deciding

00:11:11.239 --> 00:11:14.002
so you look at the low levels in visual processing

00:11:14.002 --> 00:11:16.974
for something like our image identify function

00:11:16.974 --> 00:11:22.753
it is identifying things that correspond to you know, vertical stripes, horizontal stripes, blobs

00:11:22.753 --> 00:11:27.483
other such things. Things that are actually very similar to the human primary visual cortex

00:11:27.483 --> 00:11:30.130
and things for which we as humans have pretty good names

00:11:30.130 --> 00:11:33.044
you know, it's vertical stuff, it's horizontal stuff, things like this

00:11:33.044 --> 00:11:38.202
as you go to higher levels in the system it is making distincitons,

00:11:38.202 --> 00:11:41.527
it's deciding, you know, it's playing some analog of twenty questions

00:11:41.527 --> 00:11:45.465
or something effectively, It is deciding which mineral it is

00:11:45.465 --> 00:11:53.693
it's making a distinction, this class of things are distinguished by the fact they have weird crinkled boundries

00:11:53.693 --> 00:11:56.158
that have these characteristics and so on

00:11:56.158 --> 00:12:00.133
something that we, where as these things have a different form

00:12:00.133 --> 00:12:03.822
It's making distinctions but we don't have words for those distinctions

00:12:03.822 --> 00:12:06.397
we have words for some of the lower level distinctions,

00:12:06.397 --> 00:12:09.183
we don't have words for these intermediate distinctions

00:12:09.183 --> 00:12:13.526
yet in some of these systems these words can be very useful

00:12:13.526 --> 00:12:17.078
in making distinctions so to speak, so they're things for which we could have words,

00:12:17.078 --> 00:12:23.368
but the actual evolution of our species and our language and so on hasn't given us words for those things

00:12:23.368 --> 00:12:32.870
and so I have this sort of crazy pretentious name that I came up for this, post-linguistic emergent concepts or plecs

00:12:32.870 --> 00:12:39.664
for these kinds of things that are emergent ideas that essentially AI's will have

00:12:39.664 --> 00:12:42.550
that allow them to distinguish things about the world

00:12:42.550 --> 00:12:46.729
so one scenario that I have to say I am slightly curious about,

00:12:46.729 --> 00:12:50.290
is that there may be that there are lots of these distinctions that can be made

00:12:50.290 --> 00:12:55.166
and when you start learning things in  much bigger systems, there will be these kinds of plecs,

00:12:55.166 --> 00:13:02.707
the systems use to make distinctions between things and in  a sense there will be a big dialogue of plecs

00:13:02.707 --> 00:13:06.190
among the AI's that us humans just simply can't understand

00:13:06.190 --> 00:13:09.886
in other words our language advances a certain rate we add a certain number of words

00:13:09.886 --> 00:13:12.189
that deal with certain kinds of distinctions that we didn't have before,

00:13:12.189 --> 00:13:16.827
you know there are plenty of things we talk about now in technology that we weren't able to talk about before

00:13:16.827 --> 00:13:20.803
but our language advances at a quite slow rate

00:13:20.803 --> 00:13:26.236
and the question is, will we understand what happens in any if these systems?

00:13:26.236 --> 00:13:29.383
Well the answer is ultimately, no we won't understand it

00:13:29.383 --> 00:13:32.368
but that's actually nothing new, because when we look at nature

00:13:32.368 --> 00:13:35.564
there are all sorts of things that happen in nature that we don't understand

00:13:35.564 --> 00:13:37.621
we can have little pieces of what's going on

00:13:37.621 --> 00:13:41.081
we can describe using simple physical laws and things like this

00:13:41.081 --> 00:13:43.781
but a lot of what goes on, we can't understand

00:13:43.781 --> 00:13:48.725
and even for theoretical reasons we expect that they'll be computational reducibility

00:13:48.725 --> 00:13:52.405
that we'll have to actually simulate each step in order to find out what happens

00:13:56.574 --> 00:13:58.719
So a few thoughts about

00:14:05.445 --> 00:14:06.914
This is the kind of thing I like to think about

00:14:06.914 --> 00:14:12.290
There are several other things, one thing I've been interested in

00:14:12.290 --> 00:14:16.169
is how do we take human natural language

00:14:16.169 --> 00:14:21.980
so in Wolfram Language we've tried to formalize talking about lots of kinds of things in the world

00:14:21.980 --> 00:14:26.309
but we haven't fully formalized everyday discourse about things

00:14:26.309 --> 00:14:29.641
It's actually important to formalize that kind of everyday discourse

00:14:29.641 --> 00:14:35.868
because it's how people want to talk to their cars and thermostats and other kinds of other things like that

00:14:35.868 --> 00:14:39.756
there's more kinds of discourse that we have to be able to deal with

00:14:39.756 --> 00:14:44.603
so I've been interested in how do we take

00:14:45.804 --> 00:14:50.383
so for example we have a predicate like x is greater than seven

00:14:50.383 --> 00:14:55.879
and so we say there are other kinds of predicates

00:14:55.879 --> 00:15:00.280
that exist in human language like, I like chocolate, or something

00:15:00.280 --> 00:15:04.262
that is in sense a predicate, a different kind of predicate than x is greater than 7

00:15:04.262 --> 00:15:10.789
but so I've been interested in kind of deconstructing the types of meaning that exist

00:15:10.789 --> 00:15:17.140
in everyday human language, in everyday discourse and trying to find formalizations of them

00:15:17.140 --> 00:15:20.666
the same kind of way that we found formalizations in Wolfram  Language for lots of other things

00:15:20.666 --> 00:15:24.123
and actually, that subject is kind of interesting because

00:15:24.123 --> 00:15:32.955
it has a long history but mostly in ancient history, particularly in the 1600's there was a lot of work done on so called philosophical languages

00:15:32.955 --> 00:15:38.738
it's kind of fun to look back at the works of a chap called John Wilkins for example,

00:15:38.738 --> 00:15:45.214
who had a whole book on the philosophical language which is kind of a formalization of everyday discourse

00:15:45.214 --> 00:15:49.393
at the time, and it's actually sort of satisfying in a way because a lot has changed

00:15:49.393 --> 00:15:55.801
since the 1600's and there are a lot of kinds of things that were particularly about the brutality if life

00:15:55.801 --> 00:16:01.281
in the 1600's so to speak, we can just get rid of that whole section so to speak

00:16:01.281 --> 00:16:09.234
um the semantic framework, and there's also a lot of stuff which was just completely unknown in the 1600's

00:16:09.234 --> 00:16:13.049
mostly to do with technology and so on and also to some extent human orginizations

00:16:13.049 --> 00:16:17.493
that can be added, which my final comment on this

00:16:17.493 --> 00:16:19.546
there's more I could say about it

00:16:19.546 --> 00:16:21.983
but final comment on this is

00:16:21.983 --> 00:16:26.572
you know, one of the  things that's a concern is you look at automating lots of kinds of things

00:16:26.572 --> 00:16:29.738
and you ask well what will the humans actually do when all the stuff is automated

00:16:29.738 --> 00:16:38.626
the fact is in the next many kinds of activities that involve manual labor and things like this have been automated

00:16:38.626 --> 00:16:47.026
in most parts of the world,  and a lot of white collar professional kinds of activities will get automated

00:16:47.026 --> 00:16:52.668
in the next decade or two I suspect, and the question is, what do the humans do at that point?

00:16:52.668 --> 00:16:56.959
And I would say the number one thing is the humans define the goals

00:16:56.959 --> 00:17:00.721
because that's something that in some theoretical sense machines can't do

00:17:00.721 --> 00:17:05.217
but some other sense, somewhat more cynically, well the humans just play video games

